  "worked_solution": "Applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(g(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$",
